{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Xz54R8fLBUFuMDerTBDrzd","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /tmp/harvest-crust-HH6s7V/project/src/storage_mgr.rs has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b56e94b9-71d8-4e9b-996f-5bb9f9cbab5e","uuid":"9686b0c5-dfbc-42fc-b44f-9b1f105668c6","timestamp":"2026-05-29T12:26:41.407Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/tmp/harvest-crust-HH6s7V/project/src/storage_mgr.rs","oldString":"use crate::dberror::{PAGE_SIZE, RC};\nuse crate::tables::{Schema, Value};\nuse std::cell::RefCell;","newString":"use crate::dberror::{PAGE_SIZE, RC};\n#[allow(unused_imports)]\nuse crate::tables::{Schema, Value};\nuse std::cell::RefCell;","originalFile":"use crate::dberror::{PAGE_SIZE, RC};\nuse crate::tables::{Schema, Value};\nuse std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::fs::{File, OpenOptions};\nuse std::io::{Read, Seek, SeekFrom, Write};\n\npub struct SM_FileHandle {\n    pub file_name: String,\n    pub total_num_pages: i32,\n    pub cur_page_pos: i32,\n    pub mgmt_info: Option<Box<dyn std::any::Any>>,\n}\n\npub type SM_PageHandle = String;\n\npub(crate) struct FileMgmt {\n    pub file: RefCell<File>,\n}\n\nfn page_size() -> usize {\n    PAGE_SIZE as usize\n}\n\n/// Helper: construct a String of the given length filled with NUL bytes\npub(crate) fn make_zero_page() -> String {\n    // SAFETY: All-zero bytes are valid UTF-8.\n    String::from_utf8(vec![0u8; page_size()]).unwrap()\n}\n\n/// Helper: convert an arbitrary byte slice to String. Uses unsafe because the\n/// C interface stores arbitrary binary page data inside the SM_PageHandle\n/// (typed `String`), which would not generally be valid UTF-8. The signature\n/// of the public Rust API forces us to use String here.\npub(crate) fn bytes_to_handle_string(bytes: Vec<u8>) -> String {\n    // SAFETY: String layout matches Vec<u8>; we accept the trade-off of\n    // potentially non-UTF-8 content because the public API mandates String.\n    unsafe { String::from_utf8_unchecked(bytes) }\n}\n\npub(crate) fn handle_string_as_bytes_mut(s: &mut String) -> &mut [u8] {\n    // SAFETY: We may write non-UTF-8 binary data here. The String storage\n    // is just a heap buffer of bytes.\n    unsafe { s.as_mut_vec().as_mut_slice() }\n}\n\npub fn init_storage_manager() {\n    // No-op: the C version sets a global pageFile to NULL.\n}\n\npub fn create_page_file(file_name: &str) -> RC {\n    let file = match OpenOptions::new()\n        .create(true)\n        .read(true)\n        .write(true)\n        .truncate(true)\n        .open(file_name)\n    {\n        Ok(f) => f,\n        Err(_) => return RC::FileNotFound,\n    };\n    let empty = vec![0u8; page_size()];\n    let mut f = file;\n    if f.write_all(&empty).is_err() {\n        return RC::WriteFailed;\n    }\n    if f.flush().is_err() {\n        return RC::WriteFailed;\n    }\n    RC::Ok\n}\n\npub fn open_page_file(file_name: &str, f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle) -> RC {\n    let file = match OpenOptions::new().read(true).write(true).open(file_name) {\n        Ok(f) => f,\n        Err(_) => return RC::FileNotFound,\n    };\n    let mut f = file;\n    let mut header = vec![0u8; page_size()];\n    if f.read_exact(&mut header).is_err() {\n        return RC::ReadFailed;\n    }\n    // Parse total num pages from the header (atoi-like behavior).\n    let header_str: String = header\n        .iter()\n        .take_while(|&&b| b != 0)\n        .map(|&b| b as char)\n        .collect();\n    let total_num_pages: i32 = parse_atoi(&header_str);\n\n    f_handle.file_name = file_name.to_string();\n    f_handle.total_num_pages = total_num_pages;\n    f_handle.cur_page_pos = 0;\n    f_handle.mgmt_info = Some(Box::new(FileMgmt {\n        file: RefCell::new(f),\n    }));\n    RC::Ok\n}\n\nfn parse_atoi(s: &str) -> i32 {\n    let s = s.trim_start();\n    let mut chars = s.chars().peekable();\n    let mut sign = 1i64;\n    if let Some(&c) = chars.peek() {\n        if c == '-' {\n            sign = -1;\n            chars.next();\n        } else if c == '+' {\n            chars.next();\n        }\n    }\n    let mut n: i64 = 0;\n    for c in chars {\n        if let Some(d) = c.to_digit(10) {\n            n = n.saturating_mul(10).saturating_add(d as i64);\n        } else {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    (sign * n) as i32\n}\n\nfn get_file_mut<'a>(f_handle: &'a SM_FileHandle) -> Option<&'a FileMgmt> {\n    f_handle\n        .mgmt_info\n        .as_ref()\n        .and_then(|b| b.downcast_ref::<FileMgmt>())\n}\n\npub fn close_page_file(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle) -> RC {\n    let mgmt = match f_handle.mgmt_info.as_ref() {\n        Some(b) => match b.downcast_ref::<FileMgmt>() {\n            Some(m) => m,\n            None => return RC::FileHandleNotInit,\n        },\n        None => return RC::FileHandleNotInit,\n    };\n\n    // Write the header containing the total number of pages.\n    let mut header = vec![0u8; page_size()];\n    let total_str = f_handle.total_num_pages.to_string();\n    let bytes = total_str.as_bytes();\n    let n = bytes.len().min(header.len());\n    header[..n].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..n]);\n\n    {\n        let mut file = mgmt.file.borrow_mut();\n        if file.seek(SeekFrom::Start(0)).is_err() {\n            return RC::SeekFailed;\n        }\n        if file.write_all(&header).is_err() {\n            return RC::WriteFailed;\n        }\n        if file.flush().is_err() {\n            return RC::WriteFailed;\n        }\n    }\n    f_handle.mgmt_info = None;\n    RC::Ok\n}\n\npub fn destroy_page_file(file_name: &str) -> RC {\n    for _ in 0..3 {\n        if std::fs::remove_file(file_name).is_ok() {\n            return RC::Ok;\n        }\n    }\n    RC::DestroyFailed\n}\n\nfn ensure_handle_capacity(mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) {\n    let needed = page_size();\n    if mem_page.len() < needed {\n        let mut bytes: Vec<u8> = std::mem::take(mem_page).into_bytes();\n        bytes.resize(needed, 0);\n        *mem_page = bytes_to_handle_string(bytes);\n    }\n}\n\npub fn read_block(page_num: i32, f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    if page_num < 0 || page_num >= f_handle.total_num_pages {\n        return RC::ReadNonExistingPage;\n    }\n    let mgmt = match get_file_mut(f_handle) {\n        Some(m) => m,\n        None => return RC::FileHandleNotInit,\n    };\n    let offset = (page_num as u64 + 1) * page_size() as u64;\n    {\n        let mut file = mgmt.file.borrow_mut();\n        if file.seek(SeekFrom::Start(offset)).is_err() {\n            return RC::SeekFailed;\n        }\n        ensure_handle_capacity(mem_page);\n        let buf = handle_string_as_bytes_mut(mem_page);\n        if file.read_exact(&mut buf[..page_size()]).is_err() {\n            return RC::ReadFailed;\n        }\n    }\n    f_handle.cur_page_pos = page_num;\n    RC::Ok\n}\n\npub fn get_block_pos(f_handle: &SM_FileHandle) -> i32 {\n    f_handle.cur_page_pos\n}\n\npub fn read_first_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    read_block(0, f_handle, mem_page)\n}\n\npub fn read_previous_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    let page_num = f_handle.cur_page_pos - 1;\n    if page_num < 0 {\n        return RC::ReadNonExistingPage;\n    }\n    read_block(page_num, f_handle, mem_page)\n}\n\npub fn read_current_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    if f_handle.mgmt_info.is_none() {\n        return RC::FileHandleNotInit;\n    }\n    let pn = f_handle.cur_page_pos;\n    if pn < 0 || pn >= f_handle.total_num_pages {\n        return RC::ReadNonExistingPage;\n    }\n    read_block(pn, f_handle, mem_page)\n}\n\npub fn read_next_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    let page_num = f_handle.cur_page_pos + 1;\n    read_block(page_num, f_handle, mem_page)\n}\n\npub fn read_last_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &mut SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    let pn = f_handle.total_num_pages - 1;\n    if pn < 0 {\n        return RC::ReadNonExistingPage;\n    }\n    read_block(pn, f_handle, mem_page)\n}\n\npub fn write_block(page_num: i32, f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    if page_num < 0 {\n        return RC::WriteFailed;\n    }\n    if f_handle.mgmt_info.is_none() {\n        return RC::FileNotFound;\n    }\n    let offset = (page_num as u64 + 1) * page_size() as u64;\n    let mut increment_pages = false;\n    {\n        let mgmt = match get_file_mut(f_handle) {\n            Some(m) => m,\n            None => return RC::FileNotFound,\n        };\n        let mut file = mgmt.file.borrow_mut();\n        let file_size = match file.seek(SeekFrom::End(0)) {\n            Ok(p) => p,\n            Err(_) => return RC::SeekFailed,\n        };\n        if offset > file_size {\n            if page_num == f_handle.total_num_pages {\n                if file.seek(SeekFrom::Start(file_size)).is_err() {\n                    return RC::SeekFailed;\n                }\n                let pad = vec![0u8; (offset - file_size) as usize];\n                if file.write_all(&pad).is_err() {\n                    return RC::WriteFailed;\n                }\n                increment_pages = true;\n            } else {\n                return RC::WriteFailed;\n            }\n        }\n        if file.seek(SeekFrom::Start(offset)).is_err() {\n            return RC::SeekFailed;\n        }\n        let bytes = mem_page.as_bytes();\n        let n = page_size().min(bytes.len());\n        if file.write_all(&bytes[..n]).is_err() {\n            return RC::WriteFailed;\n        }\n        if n < page_size() {\n            let pad = vec![0u8; page_size() - n];\n            if file.write_all(&pad).is_err() {\n                return RC::WriteFailed;\n            }\n        }\n        if file.flush().is_err() {\n            return RC::WriteFailed;\n        }\n    }\n    if increment_pages {\n        f_handle.total_num_pages += 1;\n    }\n    f_handle.cur_page_pos = page_num;\n    RC::Ok\n}\n\npub fn write_current_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle, mem_page: &SM_PageHandle) -> RC {\n    let pn = f_handle.cur_page_pos;\n    if pn < 0 || pn >= f_handle.total_num_pages {\n        return RC::WriteFailed;\n    }\n    write_block(pn, f_handle, mem_page)\n}\n\npub fn append_empty_block(f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle) -> RC {\n    let mgmt = match get_file_mut(f_handle) {\n        Some(m) => m,\n        None => return RC::FileHandleNotInit,\n    };\n    {\n        let mut file = mgmt.file.borrow_mut();\n        if file.seek(SeekFrom::End(0)).is_err() {\n            return RC::SeekFailed;\n        }\n        let pad = vec![0u8; page_size()];\n        if file.write_all(&pad).is_err() {\n            return RC::WriteFailed;\n        }\n        if file.flush().is_err() {\n            return RC::WriteFailed;\n        }\n    }\n    f_handle.total_num_pages += 1;\n    f_handle.cur_page_pos = f_handle.total_num_pages - 1;\n    RC::Ok\n}\n\npub fn ensure_capacity(number_of_pages: i32, f_handle: &mut SM_FileHandle) -> RC {\n    if f_handle.mgmt_info.is_none() {\n        return RC::FileHandleNotInit;\n    }\n    let active = f_handle.total_num_pages;\n    if number_of_pages <= active {\n        return RC::Ok;\n    }\n    let increment = number_of_pages - active;\n    for _ in 0..increment {\n        let rc = append_empty_block(f_handle);\n        if rc != RC::Ok {\n            return rc;\n        }\n    }\n    RC::Ok\n}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":4,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":[" use crate::dberror::{PAGE_SIZE, RC};","+#[allow(unused_imports)]"," use crate::tables::{Schema, Value};"," use std::cell::RefCell;"," use std::fs::{File, OpenOptions};"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_a7cglrwlfeus5ccszir7o4qjmloxu5dnd3buecu3lut4lkcyfsta","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me trace through some test scenarios to validate correctness. Let me check the test_expr.c case for serialization:\n\nTest `serializeValue(stringToValue(\"i10\"))` → \"10\". My `string_to_value(\"i10\")` returns IntV(10), then `serialize_value` returns `format!(\"{}\", 10)` = \"10\". ✓\n\nTest `serializeValue(stringToValue(\"f5.3\"))` → \"5.300000\". My code returns FloatV(5.3) and format!(\"{:.6}\", 5.3) = \"5.300000\". ✓\n\nTest `serializeValue(stringToValue(\"sHello World\"))` → \"Hello World\". My code: prefix='s', rest=\"Hello World\", returns StringV(\"Hello World\"). ✓\n\nTest `serializeValue(stringToValue(\"bt\"))` → \"true\". My code: prefix='b', rest=\"t\", starts_with('t') → BoolV(true), formats as \"true\". ✓\n\nTest `valueEquals` for \"10 = 10\" → true. My code: both IntV(10), discriminants match, eq=true, BoolV(true). ✓\n\nTest `boolNot(stringToValue(\"bf\"), result)`. stringToValue(\"bf\") returns BoolV(false), bool_not returns BoolV(!false)=BoolV(true). ✓\n\nNow let me think about test_assign3_1.c: testInsertManyRecords writes 10000 records. Let me check the page calculation:\n- recSize = ?: schema has int(a), string(4)(b), int(c). Sum = 4+4+4=12, no padding needed. So rec_size=12.\n- slots per page = (4096 - 32) / (12+2) = 4064/14 = 290 slots per page.\n- 10000 records = ~35 pages.\n\nIn `init_buffer_pool` we open the file (3 pages). When pinning pages > 2, we need to read/write. Looking at my buffer_mgr's pin_page in the third branch: if buffer is full and another page is requested, the eviction loop runs. But the C version reads `readBlock(pageNum, ...)` from disk. If the file doesn't have enough pages, `readBlock` returns `RC_READ_NON_EXISTING_PAGE`.\n\nLooking at the C code more carefully: when `readBlock` fails (returns -ve or otherwise), it still proceeds anyway. The logic appears to be: even if the page doesn't exist on disk, allocate a fresh page slot and use it.\n\nWait, in my buffer_mgr's `pin_page_inner`, when the file doesn't have the requested page, `read_block` returns `RC::ReadNonExistingPage` and `handle_buf` stays as zeros. We continue with zeros. That's fine.\n\nBut there's an issue: when `insert_record` writes to page N, and the file only has 1 page, the buffer's dirty page eventually gets written via `write_block` which extends the file. `write_block` only allows extending by 1 page at a time (`page_num == total_num_pages`). Let me check: in `force_flush_pool`, we call `ensure_capacity(pagenum+1)` first, then `write_block`. ensure_capacity grows the file to required size by appending empty blocks. So writes to high page numbers will extend the file ahead of time. Good.\n\nBut wait — for inserts, the first_free_page_num starts at 1. We never actually write to page 0 except in createTable/closeTable for the table header. And when we `pin_page(0)` we might find it's still in pool. The tests open and re-open tables.\n\nLet me trace `createTable` -> `openTable` -> `insertRecord` paths:\n\n1. createTable: creates 1-page file. Initializes buffer pool (3 pages, FIFO). pinPage(0) reads page 0 from file (zeros), writes header bytes, marks dirty, unpins, shutdown writes back to disk via `force_flush` style.\n\nActually, my `shutdown_buffer_pool` calls `write_dirty_pages` which writes dirty pages back. So page 0 gets written back. ✓\n\n2. openTable: reads page 0, parses schema. Pins page 0, then unpins. closes - I also call `shutdown_buffer_pool` in close_table - which writes back any dirty pages.\n\n3. insertRecord: pins firstFreePageNum=1, but page 1 doesn't exist on disk yet. `pin_page` calls `readBlock(1, ...)` which fails because total_num_pages=1. handle_buf stays zeros, we install in slot 0. We modify it, mark dirty, unpin.\n\nWait - there's a subtle issue. The C code does this:\n```c\nread_code = readBlock(pageNum, &buffer_pool->fhl, page_handle);\nif (read_code >= 0) {\n   ...\n}\n```\nNote `read_code >= 0` — RC values are positive integers, so all RCs are >= 0. So this branch is always entered. The `read_code` from readBlock returning RC_READ_NON_EXISTING_PAGE (4) is `>= 0`, so the page is treated as if read OK. Yet the pageData for that handle would still contain whatever was in the calloc'd buffer (zeros). So the C code uses zero bytes.\n\nMy Rust code does the same: if read_block fails, handle_buf stays zeros. ✓\n\n4. After enough inserts the buffer pool fills (3 pages). Eviction kicks in.\n\nThe eviction in pin_page_inner needs to actually write the dirty page to disk before evicting. The C code in this branch calls `ensureCapacity` and `writeBlock`, which our code mirrors. But note: the C version pre-reads `pageNum` from disk before searching for an eviction victim. Our code does too.\n\nLet me also worry about: in `pin_page_inner` for the \"free space available\" branch, we use `bp.numread_inc()` — which is just a wrapper for incrementing num_read. Good.\n\nBut wait: I set `bp.fix_count[memory_address] += 1` AFTER calling `numread_inc()`. The C code also increments fix_count in this slot. ✓\n\n5. closeTable then reopen+closeTable for getRecord tests. Looking at next() behavior with `firstDataPageNum = -1`:\n\nWait, in the C code, `firstDataPageNum` is initialized to -1 and never set to anything else! That means scan starts at currentPageNum = -1. `getRecord(rel, {-1, slot}, ...)` — since slot >= 0 and slot < slotsPerRecord, but page is -1. C's `pin_page` with pageNum=-1 would fail. My Rust pin_page would too (it returns Error in start of pin_page_inner... actually no, it would call read_block(-1) which returns RC::ReadNonExistingPage).\n\nLooking again at C scan/next logic:\n```c\nscanMgr->currentSlotNum++;  // -1 + 1 = 0\nif (scanMgr->currentSlotNum >= slotsPerPage) {\n   scanMgr->currentPageNum++;\n   scanMgr->currentSlotNum = 0;\n}\nRID currentRID = {.page = scanMgr->currentPageNum, .slot = scanMgr->currentSlotNum};\nRC recordStatus = getRecord(tableData, currentRID, record);\n```\n\nSo currentPageNum stays at -1 for the first slotsPerPage iterations. Yet getRecord(-1, slot) — let me check what happens.\n\n`pinPage(bp, ph, -1)` — looking at C buffer_mgr, the `void_page = TRUE` check first. If void_page, we go to \"free space available\" branch. There: `read_code = readBlock(-1, ...)` returns RC_READ_NON_EXISTING_PAGE. Then `if (read_code >= 0)` is true. Memory address gets set, page is added to pool with pageNum=-1. So the function returns OK. Then getRecord checks `pageData[pos] != 'Y'`. The data is zero, so 0 != 'Y' is true, returns RC_RECORD_NOT_FOUND. So the scan loop just keeps iterating, scan_index stays the same, but it says \"if (scanMgr->scanIndex >= scanMgr->totalEntries)\" which is 0 >= total. After enough iterations on page -1, currentSlotNum reaches slotsPerPage, then currentPageNum becomes 0. getRecord(0, 0) — page 0 is the table header page, also won't have 'Y' marker. Then page 1 onwards has actual records.\n\nWait, but `firstFreePageNum = 1` and inserts go starting at page 1. So page 1 has slot identifier 'Y' set. ✓\n\nBut the scan starts at page -1, then 0, then 1... the loop terminates when scan_index >= total_entries. So after 10 records inserted (one page), the scan would find them on page 1.\n\nOh wait, the scan_index only increments when `getRecord == RC_OK`, which happens when 'Y' is found. The loop's outer condition `if (scanMgr->scanIndex >= scanMgr->totalEntries) return RC_RM_NO_MORE_TUPLES`. So it'll iterate until total tuples found.\n\nOK so we're consistent with C. But my Rust pin_page might behave differently than C for negative page_num. Let me re-check:\n\nIn my `pin_page_inner`, the \"free space available\" branch calls `read_block(page_num, ...)`. My `read_block` returns `RC::ReadNonExistingPage` for negative page_num — good. Then we ignore the error. Then we install at slot `memory_address`, set `pagenum[mem]=-1`. Subsequent calls will find this entry: `bp.pagenum[i] == page_num (which is -1)` matches. So pin_page would find page -1 in cache from now on.\n\nBut wait - in the search loop: `for i in 0..used { if bp.pagenum[i] == page_num }`. If `page_num=-1` matches an existing -1 entry, we return that slot's data. The data is all zeros, so getRecord sees 0 != b'Y', returns RecordNotFound. Good.\n\nOK this should work. But let me also worry: when we eventually pin page 1, the buffer has -1, 0 already, plus we'd add 1. But what about page identifiers: the data for page 0 was actually written by `createTable` as the table header (not zeros). When openTable runs, it pins page 0 and reads the header. Then unpins. Then in `insertRecord`, pinning firstFreePageNum=1 — buffer has [0]. Pin returns and slot index 1 holds page 1. After enough inserts, buffer might have 0,1,2,3 etc.\n\nHmm but we initialize buffer pool with 3 pages. So after a few different page numbers come in, eviction happens. When evicting page 0 (which has the dirty table header data after openTable wrote it back? No — openTable doesn't dirty page 0. So it's not dirty at eviction time, no write back, just discarded).\n\nBut wait — I see a problem. In `closeTable`, I write the table header back with markDirty. So the page 0 buffer is marked dirty before shutdown. But in `openTable`, do I markDirty? Let me check my code. I don't markDirty in open_table. Good.\n\nBut there's still an issue: in `openTable`, I `pin_page(0)`. Then I call `unpin_page(0)`. Then later in close_table, I pin_page(0) again to update the header. Between open and close, other operations (insert, delete, update) may pin other pages. With 3 buffer pages, they can coexist OK if total accessed pages <= 3. Otherwise, page 0 might be evicted. When closeTable re-pins page 0, it would read from disk (which has the original header). Then we update with new totalTuples etc.\n\nNow testInsertManyRecords inserts 10